**(1) Offense.** A person is guilty of an offense if he—

(1) being a federal public servant, hires, or recruits, for employment in the government of the United States, or of any inferior jurisdiction or territory, any person knowing the person is an alien;

(2) being a federal public servant, after hiring a person for employment, to continue to employ the person in the government of the United States, or of any inferior jurisdiction or territory, knowing the person is (or has become) an alien;

(3) being an alien, obtains from any federal public servant, employment in the government of the United States, or of any inferior jurisdiction or territory, or continues in such employment knowing he is (or has become) an alien.

**(2) Grading.** An offense described in this section is a Class A misdemeanor. If a person acts surreptitiously in unlawful employment or attempts to conceal the fact he is an alien, it is a Class C felony.

**(3) Definitions**. As used in this section, the term “alien” means any person not a citizen or national of the United States.

**(1) Failure to Appear or to Produce.** A person who has been lawfully ordered to appear at a specific time and place to testify or to produce information in an official proceeding is guilty of a Class A misdemeanor if, without lawful privilege, he fails to appear or to produce the information at that time and place.

‌

**(2) Refusal to be Sworn.** A person attending an official proceeding is guilty of a Class A misdemeanor if, without lawful privilege, he fails to comply with a lawful order:

(a) to occupy or remain at the designated place from which he is to testify as a witness in such proceeding; or

(b) to be sworn or to make equivalent affirmation as a witness in such proceeding.

‌

**(3) Defenses.** It is a defense to a prosecution under this section that the defendant:

(a) was prevented from appearing at the specified time and place or unable to produce the information because of circumstances to the creation of which he did not contribute in reckless disregard of the requirement to appear or to produce; or

(b) complied with the order before his failure to do so substantially affected the proceeding.

‌

**(4) Definitions.** In this section:

(a) “official proceeding means

(i) an official proceeding before a judge or court of the United States, a United States magistrate, or a federal grand jury;

(ii) an official proceeding before Congress;

(iii) a federal official proceeding in which pursuant to lawful authority a court orders attendance or the production of information;

(iv) an official proceeding before an authorized agency;

(v) an official proceeding which otherwise is made expressly subject to this section;

(b) “authorized agency” means an agency authorized by federal statute to issue subpoenas or similar process supported by the sanctions of this section;

(c) “official proceeding before Congress” means an inquiry authorized before either House or any joint committee established by a joint or concurrent resolution of the two Houses of Congress or any committee or subcommittee of either House of Congress;

(d) “information” means a book, paper, document, record, or other tangible object.

